,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30,$ and let $f(x)$ be a polynomial such that\n\\[g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42.\\]Find the sum of all possible values of $f(10^{100}).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\), we start by analyzing the given equation \\( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) where \\( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\( g(f(x)) \\) in terms of \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30. \\]\n\nWe know that:\n\\[ (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30 = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nTo find \\( f(x) \\), we need to determine the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\). Let's denote the roots of this polynomial by \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4 \\). Then, we have:\n\\[ (f(x) - r_1)(f(x) - r_2)(f(x) - r_3)(f(x) - r_4) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nSince \\( g(x) = (x-5)(x-6) \\), it follows that \\( f(x) \\) must be one of the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) shifted by 5 or 6. Therefore, the possible values of \\( f(x) \\) are \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4, r_1+5, r_2+5, r_3+5, r_4+5, r_1+6, r_2+6, r_3+6, r_4+6 \\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e1f15de308c07e1575e62be64dbeb055cb640a47e8eada4fe3175eda0493da80"},"seconds":64.91177225112915,"seed":1014319670250497837,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we can use the Rational Root Theorem, which suggests that any rational root of the polynomial is a factor of the constant term (42) divided by a factor of the leading coefficient (1). The factors of 42 are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 6, \\pm 7, \\pm 14, \\pm 21, \\pm 42 \\). Testing these factors, we find that:\n\n\\[ 1^4 - 14 \\cdot 1^3 + 62 \\cdot 1^2 - 91 \\cdot 1 + 42 = 1 - 14 + 62 - 91 + 42 = 0, \\]\nso \\( x = 1 \\) is a root. Using synthetic division to divide \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) by \\( x - 1 \\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrrr}\n1 & 1 & -14 & 62 & -91 & 42 \\\\\n  &   &  1 & -13 & 49 & -42 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 1 & -13 & 49 & -42 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe quotient is \\( x^3 - 13x^2 + 49x - 42 \\). Testing the factors again, we find that \\( x = 2 \\) is a root of this quotient. Using synthetic division again, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrr}\n2 & 1 & -13 & 49 & -42 \\\\\n  &   &  2 & -22 & 54 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 1 & -11 & 27 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe quotient is \\( x^2 - 11x + 21 \\). The roots of this quadratic are found using the quadratic formula:\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 \\pm \\sqrt{121 - 84}}{2} = \\frac{11 \\pm \\sqrt{37}}{2}. \\]\nThus, the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) are \\( 1, 2, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2}, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} \\).\n\nSince \\( g(f(x)) = (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30 \\), the possible values for \\( f(x) \\) are the roots of the polynomial shifted by 5 or 6. Therefore, the possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) are:\n\\[ 1, 2, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2}, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2}, 6, 7, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5, 7, 8, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6. \\]\n\nSumming these values:\n\\[ (1 + 2 + \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6 + 7 + \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5 + \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5 + 7 + 8 + \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6 + \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6) = 84. \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is \\(\\boxed{84}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{84}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we can use the Rational Root Theorem, which suggests that any rational root of the polynomial is a factor of the constant term (42) divided by a factor of the leading coefficient (1). The factors of 42 are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 6, \\pm 7, \\pm 14, \\pm 21, \\pm 42 \\). Testing these factors, we find that:\n\n\\[ 1^4 - 14 \\cdot 1^3 + 62 \\cdot 1^2 - 91 \\cdot 1 + 42 = 1 - 14 + 62 - 91 + 42 = 0, \\]\nso \\( x = 1 \\) is a root. Using synthetic division to divide \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) by \\( x - 1 \\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrrr}\n1 & 1 & -14 & 62 & -91 & 42 \\\\\n  &   &  1 & -13 & 49 & -42 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 1 & -13 & 49 & -42 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe quotient is \\( x^3 - 13x^2 + 49x - 42 \\). Testing the factors again, we find that \\( x = 2 \\) is a root of this quotient. Using synthetic division again, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrr}\n2 & 1 & -13 & 49 & -42 \\\\\n  &   &  2 & -22 & 54 \\\\\n\\hline\n  & 1 & -11 & 27 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe quotient is \\( x^2 - 11x + 21 \\). The roots of this quadratic are found using the quadratic formula:\n\\[ x = \\frac{11 \\pm \\sqrt{121 - 84}}{2} = \\frac{11 \\pm \\sqrt{37}}{2}. \\]\nThus, the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) are \\( 1, 2, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2}, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} \\).\n\nSince \\( g(f(x)) = (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30 \\), the possible values for \\( f(x) \\) are the roots of the polynomial shifted by 5 or 6. Therefore, the possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) are:\n\\[ 1, 2, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2}, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2}, 6, 7, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5, 7, 8, \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6, \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6. \\]\n\nSumming these values:\n\\[ (1 + 2 + \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6 + 7 + \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5 + \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 5 + 7 + 8 + \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6 + \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{37}}{2} + 6) = 84. \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is \\(\\boxed{84}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:58"}
